.\" dirname.1 -- maux's dirname(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text, not copied from a
.\" historic dirname(1)), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as basename.1. Rendered at
.\" build time to build/man/DIRNAME.TXT and staged on the play
.\" image as /usr/man/DIRNAME: `more /usr/man/DIRNAME`.
.TH DIRNAME 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
dirname \- return all but the last component of a pathname
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dirname
.I string
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dirname
deletes the last component of
.IR string ,
beginning with the last slash
.RB ( / )
character, after first stripping trailing slashes, and writes
the result to standard output.
.PP
If
.I string
consists entirely of slashes, a single
.B /
is printed. If, after stripping trailing slashes,
.I string
contains no slash at all, a single
.B .
is printed. If removing the last component leaves nothing but
slashes, a single
.B /
is printed.
.PP
.I String
is text, not a filesystem lookup: no file or directory named by
.I string
needs to exist, and none is opened.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/DIRNAME  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
basename(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success; 2 on a usage error (a number of
operands other than one); 1 if the result would not fit
dirname's own working buffer (96 bytes).
.SH BUGS
This is the POSIX single-operand form only; some other
implementations accept more than one
.I string
and print one line per operand.
